Harbor: with the OpenGL renderer, a swap chain in a child window presents through GDI (#94).

Presenting through OpenGL gives a window a surface of its own, laid over its top-level window without regard to
the other windows in it. On macOS a swap chain's picture then covers the windows inside its window and what
overlapping windows draw. Office builds a window from a dozen swap chains in nested child windows: the sheet's
window lies inside another window with a swap chain of its own, and was covered by it.

wined3d already presents through GDI when it has no usable drawable; that path goes through the top-level
window's surface, where windows clip each other as they should. It is now taken for every swap chain whose
window is a child window, from its first frame (a window once presented to through OpenGL keeps that surface on
top, so the choice can't be made later, when other windows turn up).

Cost: each frame is read back from the GPU. Affected: the OpenGL renderer only, which is used for Direct3D 9
and earlier, and for Direct3D 10/11 only where a bottle selects it (the default there is Vulkan). A swap chain
in a top-level window presents as before; a single swap chain in a child window worked before and now takes
the slower path too.

Test: WindowsTests/graphics/childswap.sh (stack).

--- a/dlls/wined3d/swapchain.c
+++ b/dlls/wined3d/swapchain.c
@@ -608,6 +608,21 @@
         return true;
 
     return false;
+}
+
+/* HARBOR HACK: presenting through OpenGL gives a window a surface of its own, laid over its top-level window
+ * without regard to the other windows in it: on macOS a swap chain's picture covered the windows inside its
+ * window, and what was drawn in windows overlapping it. Office builds its windows from a dozen swap chains in
+ * nested child windows (#94). Presenting through GDI goes through the top-level window's surface, where windows
+ * clip each other as they should; it costs reading each frame back.
+ * That is done for every swap chain in a child window, from its first frame: a window that has once been
+ * presented to through OpenGL keeps that surface on top, so the choice can't wait until other windows turn up.
+ * A swap chain in a top-level window presents as before. */
+static BOOL swapchain_window_is_child(HWND window)
+{
+    HWND root = GetAncestor(window, GA_ROOT);
+
+    return root && root != window;
 }
 
 static void swapchain_gl_present(struct wined3d_swapchain *swapchain,
@@ -632,6 +647,7 @@
 
     pixel_format = &wined3d_adapter_gl(swapchain->device->adapter)->pixel_formats[context_gl->pixel_format];
     if (context_gl->dc == wined3d_device_gl(swapchain->device)->backup_dc
+            || swapchain_window_is_child(swapchain->win_handle)
             || (pixel_format->swap_method != WGL_SWAP_COPY_ARB
             && swapchain_present_is_partial_copy(swapchain, dst_rect)))
     {
